A decorator that adds one method wraps a single function; to attach several from a dict, write a class decorator instead - it receives the class itself and can `setattr` in a loop:

```python
def add_methods(methods):
    def decorator(cls):
        for name, func in methods.items():
            setattr(cls, name, func)
        return cls
    return decorator

methods = {
    "walk": lambda self: print(f"{self.name} is walking"),
    "run": lambda self: print(f"{self.name} is running"),
}

@add_methods(methods)
class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

Animal("Rex").walk()  # Rex is walking
```

`setattr(cls, name, func)` binds each lambda as an instance method since it already accepts `self` as its first parameter, so it behaves exactly like a normally defined method. This class-decorator pattern handles any number of dictionary entries in a single line at class-definition time.
